      "ref": "RFC9449 §4.2 ¶14",
      "rfc": 9449,
      "section": "DPoP Proof JWT Syntax",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "A DPoP proof MAY contain other JOSE Header Parameters or claims as defined by extension, profile, or deployment-specific requirements."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9449 §4.3 ¶1",
      "rfc": 9449,
      "section": "Checking DPoP Proofs",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "To validate a DPoP proof, the receiving server MUST ensure the following:"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "How should a native app receive the authorization response?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §7 ¶1",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Receiving the Authorization Response in a Native App",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "There are several redirect URI options available to native apps for receiving the authorization response from the browser, the availability and user experience of which varies by platform."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §7 ¶2",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Receiving the Authorization Response in a Native App",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "To fully support this best practice, authorization servers MUST offer at least the three redirect URI options described in the following subsections to native apps. Native apps MAY use whichever redirect option suits their needs best, taking into account platform-specific implementation details."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §4.1 ¶7",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Authorization Flow for Native Apps Using the Browser",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "(4) Client receives the authorization code from the redirect URI."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §6 ¶1",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Initiating the Authorization Request from a Native App",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Native apps needing user authorization create an authorization request URI with the authorization code grant type per Section 4.1 of OAuth 2.0 [RFC6749], using a redirect URI capable of being received by the native app."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §6 ¶2",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Initiating the Authorization Request from a Native App",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The function of the redirect URI for a native app authorization request is similar to that of a web-based authorization request. Rather than returning the authorization response to the OAuth client's server, the redirect URI used by a native app returns the response to the app. Several options for a redirect URI that will return the authorization response to the native app in different platforms are documented in Section 7. Any redirect URI that allows the app to receive the URI and inspect its parameters is viable."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §4.1 ¶5",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Authorization Flow for Native Apps Using the Browser",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "(2) Authorization endpoint receives the authorization request, authenticates the user, and obtains authorization. Authenticating the user may involve chaining to other authentication systems."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §4.1 ¶2",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Authorization Flow for Native Apps Using the Browser",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Figure 1: Native App Authorization via an External User-Agent"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §4.1 ¶4",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Authorization Flow for Native Apps Using the Browser",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "(1) Client app opens a browser tab with the authorization request."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "Is the implicit grant still recommended?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC6749 §4.2 ¶7",
      "rfc": 6749,
      "section": "Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Figure 4: Implicit Grant Flow"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §2.1.2 ¶2",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Moreover, no standardized method for sender-constraining exists to bind access tokens to a specific client (as recommended in Section 2.2) when the access tokens are issued in the authorization response. This means that an attacker can use the leaked or stolen access token at a resource endpoint."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §8.2 ¶1",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "OAuth Implicit Grant Authorization Flow",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The OAuth 2.0 implicit grant authorization flow (defined in Section 4.2 of OAuth 2.0 [RFC6749]) generally works with the practice of performing the authorization request in the browser and receiving the authorization response via URI-based inter-app communication. However, as the implicit flow cannot be protected by PKCE [RFC7636] (which is required in Section 8.1), the use of the Implicit Flow with native apps is NOT RECOMMENDED."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC6749 §4.2 ¶8",
      "rfc": 6749,
      "section": "Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The flow illustrated in Figure 4 includes the following steps:"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC6749 §4.2 ¶15",
      "rfc": 6749,
      "section": "Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "(G) The user-agent passes the access token to the client."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §4.1.2 ¶13",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Redirect URI Validation Attacks on Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "https://attacker.example/#access_token=2YotnFZFEjr1z..."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC6749 §4.2 ¶16",
      "rfc": 6749,
      "section": "Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "See Sections 1.3.2 and 9 for background on using the implicit grant. See Sections 10.3 and 10.16 for important security considerations when using the implicit grant."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §4.1.2 ¶4",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Redirect URI Validation Attacks on Implicit Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The attack can now be conducted as follows:"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "What claims must a JWT access token contain?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC9068 §4 ¶8",
      "rfc": 9068,
      "section": "Validating JWT Access Tokens",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "* The resource server MUST validate that the \"aud\" claim contains a resource indicator value corresponding to an identifier the resource server expects for itself. The JWT access token MUST be rejected if \"aud\" does not contain a resource indicator of the current resource server as a valid audience."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9449 §4.2 ¶10",
      "rfc": 9449,
      "section": "DPoP Proof JWT Syntax",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "When the DPoP proof is used in conjunction with the presentation of an access token in protected resource access (see Section 7), the DPoP proof MUST also contain the following claim:"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9068 §4 ¶7",
      "rfc": 9068,
      "section": "Validating JWT Access Tokens",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "* The issuer identifier for the authorization server (which is typically obtained during discovery) MUST exactly match the value of the \"iss\" claim."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9068 §3 ¶9",
      "rfc": 9068,
      "section": "Requesting a JWT Access Token",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Claims:"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9449 §4.2 ¶5",
      "rfc": 9449,
      "section": "DPoP Proof JWT Syntax",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The payload of a DPoP proof MUST contain at least the following claims:"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9068 §3 ¶11",
      "rfc": 9068,
      "section": "Requesting a JWT Access Token",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Figure 2: The Header and JWT Claims Set of a JWT Access Token"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC7519 §4 ¶2",
      "rfc": 7519,
      "section": "JWT Claims",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The set of claims that a JWT must contain to be considered valid is context dependent and is outside the scope of this specification. Specific applications of JWTs will require implementations to understand and process some claims in particular ways. However, in the absence of such requirements, all claims that are not understood by implementations MUST be ignored."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9068 §4 ¶10",
      "rfc": 9068,
      "section": "Validating JWT Access Tokens",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "* The current time MUST be before the time represented by the \"exp\" claim. Implementers MAY provide for some small leeway, usually no more than a few minutes, to account for clock skew."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "When must a client use PKCE?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §4.7.1 ¶3",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Countermeasures",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "* Clients MUST ensure that the authorization server supports PKCE before using PKCE for CSRF protection. If an authorization server does not support PKCE, state or nonce MUST be used for CSRF protection."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §4.8 ¶3",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "PKCE Downgrade Attack",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The second prerequisite for this attack is that the client is not using state at all (e.g., because the client relies on PKCE for CSRF prevention) or that the client is not checking state correctly."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §6 ¶3",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Initiating the Authorization Request from a Native App",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Public native app clients MUST implement the Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE [RFC7636]) extension to OAuth, and authorization servers MUST support PKCE for such clients, for the reasons detailed in Section 8.1."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §2.1.1 ¶9",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Authorization Code Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Authorization servers MUST provide a way to detect their support for PKCE. It is RECOMMENDED for authorization servers to publish the element code_challenge_methods_supported in their Authorization Server Metadata [RFC8414] containing the supported PKCE challenge methods (which can be used by the client to detect PKCE support). Authorization servers MAY instead provide a deployment-specific way to ensure or determine PKCE support by the authorization server."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §2.1.1 ¶6",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Authorization Code Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Authorization servers MUST support PKCE [RFC7636]."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §4.8 ¶1",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "PKCE Downgrade Attack",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "An authorization server that supports PKCE but does not make its use mandatory for all flows can be susceptible to a PKCE downgrade attack."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC9700 §2.1.1 ¶5",
      "rfc": 9700,
      "section": "Authorization Code Grant",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "When using PKCE, clients SHOULD use PKCE code challenge methods that do not expose the PKCE verifier in the authorization request. Otherwise, attackers that can read the authorization request (cf. Attacker (A4) in Section 3) can break the security provided by PKCE. Currently, S256 is the only such method."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8252 §8.1 ¶6",
      "rfc": 8252,
      "section": "Protecting the Authorization Code",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "Section 6 requires that both clients and servers use PKCE for public native app clients. Authorization servers SHOULD reject authorization requests from native apps that don't use PKCE by returning an error message, as defined in Section 4.4.1 of PKCE [RFC7636]."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "How does a resource server authenticate to the token introspection endpoint?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC7662 §2.1 ¶10",
      "rfc": 7662,
      "section": "Introspection Request",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "In this example, the protected resource uses a client identifier and client secret to authenticate itself to the introspection endpoint. The protected resource also sends a token type hint indicating that it is inquiring about an access token."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC7662 §4 ¶4",
      "rfc": 7662,
      "section": "Security Considerations",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "If left unprotected and un-throttled, the introspection endpoint could present a means for an attacker to poll a series of possible token values, fishing for a valid token. To prevent this, the authorization server MUST require authentication of protected resources that need to access the introspection endpoint and SHOULD require protected resources to be specifically authorized to call the introspection endpoint. The specifics of such authentication credentials are out of scope of this specification, but commonly these credentials could take the form of any valid client authentication mechanism used with the token endpoint, an OAuth 2.0 access token, or other HTTP authorization or authentication mechanism. A single piece of software acting as both a client and a protected resource MAY reuse the same credentials between the token endpoint and the introspection endpoint, though doing so potentially conflates the activities of the client and protected resource portions of the software and the authorization server MAY require separate credentials for each mode."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC7662 §2 ¶1",
      "rfc": 7662,
      "section": "Introspection Endpoint",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The introspection endpoint is an OAuth 2.0 endpoint that takes a parameter representing an OAuth 2.0 token and returns a JSON [RFC7159] document representing the meta information surrounding the token, including whether this token is currently active. The definition of an active token is dependent upon the authorization server, but this is commonly a token that has been issued by this authorization server, is not expired, has not been revoked, and is valid for use at the protected resource making the introspection call."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC7662 §2.1 ¶6",
      "rfc": 7662,
      "section": "Introspection Request",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "For example, the following shows a protected resource calling the token introspection endpoint to query about an OAuth 2.0 bearer token. The protected resource is using a separate OAuth 2.0 bearer token to authorize this call."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC7662 §2 ¶2",
      "rfc": 7662,
      "section": "Introspection Endpoint",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "The introspection endpoint MUST be protected by a transport-layer security mechanism as described in Section 4. The means by which the protected resource discovers the location of the introspection endpoint are outside the scope of this specification."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8705 §5 ¶5",
      "rfc": 8705,
      "section": "Metadata for Mutual-TLS Endpoint Aliases",
      "figure": true,
      "text": "{\n  \"issuer\": \"https://server.example.com\",\n  \"authorization_endpoint\": \"https://server.example.com/authz\",\n  \"token_endpoint\": \"https://server.example.com/token\",\n  \"introspection_endpoint\": \"https://server.example.com/introspect\",\n  \"revocation_endpoint\": \"https://server.example.com/revo\",\n  \"jwks_uri\": \"https://server.example.com/jwks\",\n  \"response_types_supported\": [\"code\"],\n  \"response_modes_supported\": [\"fragment\",\"query\",\"form_post\"],\n  \"grant_types_supported\": [\"authorization_code\", \"refresh_token\"],\n  \"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\":\n                  [\"tls_client_auth\",\"client_secret_basic\",\"none\"],\n  \"tls_client_certificate_bound_access_tokens\": true,\n  \"mtls_endpoint_aliases\": {\n    \"token_endpoint\": \"https://mtls.example.com/token\",\n    \"revocation_endpoint\": \"https://mtls.example.com/revo\",\n    \"introspection_endpoint\": \"https://mtls.example.com/introspect\"\n  }\n}"
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8414 §2 ¶21",
      "rfc": 8414,
      "section": "Authorization Server Metadata",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "introspection_endpoint_auth_methods_supported OPTIONAL. JSON array containing a list of client authentication methods supported by this introspection endpoint. The valid client authentication method values are those registered in the IANA \"OAuth Token Endpoint Authentication Methods\" registry [IANA.OAuth.Parameters] or those registered in the IANA \"OAuth Access Token Types\" registry [IANA.OAuth.Parameters]. (These values are and will remain distinct, due to Section 7.2.) If omitted, the set of supported authentication methods MUST be determined by other means."
    },
    {
      "ref": "RFC8414 §2 ¶22",
      "rfc": 8414,
      "section": "Authorization Server Metadata",
      "figure": false,
      "text": "introspection_endpoint_auth_signing_alg_values_supported OPTIONAL. JSON array containing a list of the JWS signing algorithms (\"alg\" values) supported by the introspection endpoint for the signature on the JWT [JWT] used to authenticate the client at the introspection endpoint for the \"private_key_jwt\" and \"client_secret_jwt\" authentication methods. This metadata entry MUST be present if either of these authentication methods are specified in the \"introspection_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\" entry. No default algorithms are implied if this entry is omitted. The value \"none\" MUST NOT be used."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "question": "How do I bake sourdough bread?",
  "passages": [
    {
      "ref": "RFC9110 §B.3 ¶4",
      "rfc": 9110,
      "section": "Changes from RFC 7231",
      "figure": false,
